            <ArticleTitle>Role of dobutamine stress echocardiography for preoperative cardiac risk assessment before major vascular surgery: a diagnostic tool comes of age.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>79-91</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Cardiac complications are a major cause for perioperative mortality and morbidity. Also, the presence and severity of underlying coronary artery disease (CAD) determine long-term prognosis after successful surgery.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="AIM" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">This overview evaluates the additional value of dobutamine stress echocardiography (DSE) to common clinical cardiac risk factors and other noninvasive cardiac imaging modalities for perioperative and late cardiac prognosis.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">DSE provides theattending physician with preoperative prognostic information for perioperative and long-term prognosis for cardiac events. It also enables the selection of high risk patients for evaluation of cardiac risk reduction therapies.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">DSE is a useful tool for preoperative cardiac risk evaluation in addition to common clinical cardiac risk factors.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
